<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Harbor.Providers.OpenAiCompatible</PackageId>
    <Description>Harbor generic OpenAI-compatible LLM provider.</Description>
    <PackageTags>harbor;provider;openai;compatible</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Harbor.Providers.Shared\SsePump.cs" LinkBase="Shared" />
    <Compile Include="..\Harbor.Providers.Shared\OpenAiWire.cs" LinkBase="Shared" />
    <Compile Include="..\Harbor.Providers.Shared\OpenAiImageContent.cs" LinkBase="Shared" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- §ARCH-003: Infrastructure references Domain (Harbor.Abstractions) ONLY.
         The previous Harbor.Core facade reference was vestigial — no `using Harbor.Core.*`
         exists in this project. Removed by arch-cleaner. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harbor.Abstractions\Harbor.Abstractions.csproj"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      §ARCH-003 sweep (#910): `Microsoft.Extensions.Http` was vestigial and is
      gone. This client takes a plain `HttpClient` in its constructor
      (OpenAiCompatibleLlmClient.cs, ProviderConfig.cs) — `HttpClient` is
      System.Net.Http, the BCL — and neither `IHttpClientFactory` nor
      `AddHttpClient` appears in this project or in the three linked
      Harbor.Providers.Shared sources.

      This is the one place where removal is observable, because this project
      is IsPackable=true. Resolved against the net10.0 dependency groups of the
      real nuspecs, the reference carried 11 further packages into the pack's
      dependency group — Configuration{,.Abstractions,.Binder}, DependencyInjection,
      Diagnostics{,.Abstractions}, Logging, Options, Options.ConfigurationExtensions,
      Primitives, plus Http itself. The two that remain are Logging.Abstractions
      (direct) and DependencyInjection.Abstractions (its own dependency).

      Note this is a PACK-time claim only. The shipped binary is unchanged:
      src/Harbor.Hosting and apps/Harbor.App.Cli each carry their own
      Microsoft.Extensions.Http, and Microsoft.Extensions.Http 10.0.10 ships
      IsAotCompatible metadata, so nothing here was ever an AOT blocker.

      BLAST RADIUS, because this one is NOT cosmetic and the difference from
      the Harbor.Tui.Tests half of #910 is exactly this. Three of the seven
      projects that ProjectReference this one were resolving the package only
      THROUGH it, and lose it from their compile closure with this line gone:
      tests/Harbor.Benchmarks (3 packages), tests/Harbor.LoadTests (6), and
      tests/Harbor.Providers.Tests (9 — including the FULL
      Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection, .Logging, .Options and
      .Configuration). Checked: none of the three names IServiceCollection,
      ServiceDescriptor, Add*, BuildServiceProvider, IOptions, IConfiguration,
      ConfigurationBuilder, IHostedService, LoggerFactory, AddLogging,
      DiagnosticListener, IHttpClientFactory or AddHttpClient, in any .cs file
      or any using directive — so none of them was leaning on the leak. The
      other four consumers (Harbor.Hosting, apps/Harbor.App.Cli,
      apps/Harbor.App.Avalonia, tests/Harbor.Architecture.Tests) keep it by
      their own references or other routes.

      NOT VERIFIED BY COMPILE — `dotnet` was out of scope for this change. The
      three-consumer check above is a static nuspec walk plus a text probe; CI
      is what actually proves it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Harbor.App.Cli"/>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Harbor.Benchmarks"/>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Harbor.Providers.Tests"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
